==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|þenċean}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|ang|gmw-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|ang|gmw-pro|*þankijan}}, from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*þankijaną}}.

Cognate with {{cog|ofs|thenka}}, {{cog|osx|thenkian}}, {{cog|odt|thenken}}, {{cog|goh|denken}}, {{cog|non|þekkja}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐌰𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽}}. All ultimately from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*teng-||to think}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|þenċan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|þenċan}}

# to [[think]]
#: {{ux|ang|Iċ '''þenċe''', for þȳ iċ eom.|I '''think''', therefore I am.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Ne magon wē '''þenċan''' þæt wē seċġan ne magon.|We cannot '''think''' what we cannot say.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Betere is þæt man '''þenċe''' tō swīðe þonne tō lȳt.|It's better to '''think''' too much than too little.}}

====Usage notes====
This word refers primarily to thinking as in having thoughts. For thinking as in having an opinion, {{m|ang|wēnan}} was most often used.

